.TH PHASORSTD_STRUCT 3 "August 2026" "Phasor 4.0.0" "Phasor Standard Library 4.0.0"
.SH NAME
phasorstd_struct \- Phasor struct field and array-of-structs search functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBinclude "std/struct";\fR
.PP
.B has(struct_value, field_name)
.B struct_find(array, field_name[, value])
.B filter(array, conditions)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
This module provides functions for structs, obviously.
To inspect the actual list of field
.I names
a struct has (rather than just checking one), see \fBget_elements()\fR and
\fBget_elements_values()\fR in \fBphasorstd_type\fR(3).
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BR has (struct_value, field_name)
Check whether a struct has a given field.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBstruct_value\fR 12
A struct instance
.IP \fBfield_name\fR 12
Field name to look for
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean true if the struct has a field named \fBfield_name\fR, false otherwise
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBstruct_value\fR is not a struct, or \fBfield_name\fR is not a string
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR struct_find (array, field_name[, value])
Find the first struct in an array whose given field is present (and, optionally, equal to a
specific value).
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBarray\fR 12
Array to search. Non-struct elements are skipped rather than causing an error.
.IP \fBfield_name\fR 12
Field name each candidate struct must have
.IP \fBvalue\fR 12
Optional value the field must equal. If omitted, the first struct that simply has a field
named \fBfield_name\fR is returned, regardless of its value.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The first matching struct element, or null if no element matches (or if the array is empty)
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBarray\fR is not an array, if \fBfield_name\fR is not a string, or if more
than 3 arguments are given
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR filter (array, conditions)
Find every struct in an array that matches a set of field/value conditions, all of which must
hold.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBarray\fR 12
Array to search. Non-struct elements are skipped rather than causing an error.
.IP \fBconditions\fR 12
Array of condition structs, each with an \fBelementName\fR string field naming the field to
check, and an optional \fBvalue\fR field giving the value it must equal (if \fBvalue\fR is
omitted from a condition, the field must equal null to match)
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
A new array containing every element of \fBarray\fR that is a struct, has every field named
in \fBconditions\fR, and whose value for each of those fields equals the corresponding
condition's \fBvalue\fR. Returns an empty array if nothing matches.
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBarray\fR or \fBconditions\fR is not an array, if any element of
\fBconditions\fR is not a struct, or if a condition struct is missing an \fBelementName\fR
field or has a non-string \fBelementName\fR
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.B Checking and Finding
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/struct";

var people: any[] = [
    { name: "Alice", age: 30, role: "admin" },
    { name: "Bob", age: 25 },
    { name: "Carol", age: 30, role: "editor" }
];

puts(has(people[0], "role"));   // true
puts(has(people[1], "role"));   // false

// First struct that simply has a "role" field
var firstWithRole: any = struct_find(people, "role");
puts(firstWithRole.name);       // "Alice"

// First struct whose "age" field equals 30
var firstAge30: any = struct_find(people, "age", 30);
puts(firstAge30.name);          // "Alice"
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Filtering with Multiple Conditions
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/struct";

var people: any[] = [
    { name: "Alice", age: 30, role: "admin" },
    { name: "Bob", age: 25, role: "admin" },
    { name: "Carol", age: 30, role: "editor" }
];

// Every struct where age == 30 AND role == "admin"
var matches: any[] = filter(people, [
    { elementName: "age", value: 30 },
    { elementName: "role", value: "admin" }
]);

for (var i: i64 = 0; i < matches.length; i++) {
    puts(matches[i].name);   // "Alice"
}
.RE
.fi
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
.BR struct_find() " and " filter()
silently skip any array element that is not a struct, rather than treating it as an error or
a non-match failure \(em a mixed array of structs and other values is fine to pass in
.IP \(bu 2
.B filter()
condition structs are plain, ad hoc structs with \fBelementName\fR and \fBvalue\fR fields;
there is no declared type to include for them, just build them as struct literals
.IP \(bu 2
.B struct_find()
with only two arguments matches on field \fIpresence\fR, not on any particular value; pass a
third argument to also require a specific value
.IP \(bu 2
Field-name matching in all three functions is by exact string equality, not case-insensitive
